1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,679
Day 54: Haru-4B, a full-duplex speech model that listens and

2
00:00:05,679 --> 00:00:10,507
speaks at once, timed on CPU and on a consumer GPU.

3
00:00:10,957 --> 00:00:15,646
Haru-4B listens and speaks in the same 133 millisecond frame,

4
00:00:15,646 --> 00:00:19,874
7.5 times a second, instead of waiting for turns like a

5
00:00:19,874 --> 00:00:21,719
typical voice assistant.

6
00:00:22,169 --> 00:00:27,071
Each frame, one chunk of the user's audio goes into a Qwen3.5

7
00:00:27,071 --> 00:00:29,240
4B language model backbone.

8
00:00:29,690 --> 00:00:33,456
A small MeanFlow head turns that hidden state into the model's

9
00:00:33,456 --> 00:00:37,040
own audio in one step, while the backbone predicts the next

10
00:00:37,040 --> 00:00:38,862
text token for the same frame.

11
00:00:39,312 --> 00:00:44,393
On CPU it finished, but took about 435 milliseconds per frame,

12
00:00:44,393 --> 00:00:47,590
over three times slower than real time.

13
00:00:47,590 --> 00:00:51,524
On an RTX 5060 Ti it averaged 87.5 milliseconds,

14
00:00:51,524 --> 00:00:54,229
under the 133 millisecond target.

15
00:00:54,229 --> 00:00:58,245
Replies to the same clip changed from run to run,

16
00:00:58,245 --> 00:01:01,359
from silence to talking over the user.

17
00:01:01,809 --> 00:01:03,936
Apache 2.0, from higurashi-ai.

18
00:01:03,936 --> 00:01:08,332
Source, license and the full write-up are linked on GitHub and

19
00:01:08,332 --> 00:01:09,750
in the Zenn article.
